{"from": "site:khirbet-qana", "type": "attests", "to": "place:cana", "grade": "C", "note": "Excavations by Edwards and McCollough show a modest-sized village from c. 200 BCE to 650 CE with mikveh pools and a Roman-period synagogue-like structure - a Jewish village of the right name, period and region. Identification with Cana is debated (Kafr Kanna, Karm er-Ras, Reineh, Qana in Lebanon are the other candidates per the fetched article); the dispute is recorded here and in the claim's note. Regraded B to C by architect review, consistent with Bethany: a settlement of the right period, but its identification with Cana is debated among four candidates."}
{"from": "site:khirbet-qana", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-02-01-wedding-at-cana", "grade": "C", "note": "The excavated Roman-period village attests the setting of the wedding - a real Galilean village of the right name, period and Jewish character - not that this wedding was held there, and never the miracle. Regraded B to C by architect review, consistent with Bethany: a settlement of the right period, but its identification with Cana is debated among four candidates."}
{"from": "artifact:second-temple-stone-vessels", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-02-06-stone-jars", "grade": "B", "note": "Chalk (stone) purity vessels of the Second Temple period, found both near Jerusalem and in Galilee (Reineh, Einot Amitai - near Nazareth and the Beit Netofa valley), because stone is impervious to impurity: the same object, purpose (Jewish purification), period and region as John's six stone water jars."}
{"from": "site:hizma-stone-vessel-workshop", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-02-06-stone-jars", "grade": "B", "note": "An excavated Second Temple-period stone-vessel workshop near Jerusalem shows the vessels were industrially produced for the purity market and distributed as far as Galilee, so a village household like John's could own six such jars."}
{"from": "site:capernaum", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-02-12-capernaum-stay", "grade": "B", "note": "The excavated fishing town on the northwest shore (Hasmonean through Byzantine occupation) attests the Capernaum of 2:12 as a real, occupied town in Jesus' period; the visit itself is not independently attested."}
{"from": "site:second-temple", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-02-13-passover-pilgrimage", "grade": "B", "note": "The Second Temple was the chief place of worship that regularly attracted pilgrims for the Three Pilgrimage Festivals including Passover - the institution the 'went up to Jerusalem' of 2:13 assumes."}
{"from": "text:josephus-war-6-422-425", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-02-13-passover-pilgrimage", "grade": "C", "note": "Josephus preserves Cestius Gallus' count of the Passover pilgrims (c. 66 CE) - Passover pilgrimage to Jerusalem on a huge scale, a generation after John's scene. Redacted context of 66 CE, not 28 CE."}
{"from": "observation:grape-fig-cultivation-levant", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-02-01-wedding-at-cana", "grade": "C", "note": "Grapes (and wine) as staples of ancient Israel's agriculture: the wine a village wedding needed was locally produced. Context only; it bears on the wedding's provisions, not the miracle."}
{"from": "text:josephus-ant-15-380", "type": "attests", "to": "claim:jn-02-20-46-years", "grade": "B", "note": "Josephus dates Herod's start of the temple rebuilding to his 18th year. Arithmetic (recorded per the brief): Herod's 18th year = 20/19 BCE, so 46 years of building puts the saying of 2:20 at a Passover about 26/27-28 CE - consistent with John's early-ministry Passover. Construction was still continuing (the fetched Herod's Temple article: work continued 'possibly until the 60s CE'), which is why 'was being built' fits."}
